WEBVTT

00:00:04.051 --> 00:00:08.536
In this video you build your first network: two PCs and a switch.

00:00:08.536 --> 00:00:13.032
Open Endgeräte (end devices) on the left and click Desktop-PC.

00:00:13.032 --> 00:00:16.380
Hold the mouse button to move the device.

00:00:16.380 --> 00:00:22.927
Another PC …

00:00:22.927 --> 00:00:29.172
… and a switch from the Switches category.

00:00:29.172 --> 00:00:33.624
Now the cables: choose the cable tool, or press K.

00:00:33.624 --> 00:00:37.209
Click PC 1 first, then the switch.

00:00:37.209 --> 00:00:40.894
The same for PC 2.

00:00:40.894 --> 00:00:44.975
Back to select. Esc works too.

00:00:44.975 --> 00:00:48.706
Green dots on the cables mean: the link is up.

00:00:48.706 --> 00:00:53.495
Click PC 1. Its properties appear on the right.

00:00:53.495 --> 00:01:02.331
The IP address goes here. We use the network 192.168.1.0/24.

00:01:02.331 --> 00:01:14.622
PC 1 gets 192.168.1.10. leyoNET adds the mask 255.255.255.0.

00:01:14.622 --> 00:01:23.080
PC 2 gets 192.168.1.11. Every address may exist only once.

00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:27.890
Everything is saved automatically. No “Apply” button needed.

00:01:27.890 --> 00:01:30.988
Press M to enter the simulation.

00:01:30.988 --> 00:01:35.366
Select PC 1 and open its desktop on the right.

00:01:35.366 --> 00:01:41.846
On the command line: ping 192.168.1.11 and Enter.

00:01:41.846 --> 00:01:46.090
Four replies: your PCs reach each other!

00:01:46.090 --> 00:01:49.658
Let's close the desktop and look at the packet list.

00:01:49.658 --> 00:01:56.632
First PC 1 asks for the MAC address with ARP, then ICMP follows, the ping.

00:01:56.632 --> 00:02:01.108
Clicking a row shows the packet layer by layer.

00:02:01.108 --> 00:02:06.865
No success? Check in this order: cable, IP address, mask.

00:02:06.865 --> 00:02:10.981
In the next video you solve exercises and work in a class.
